sqly - run SQL against CSV, TSV, LTSV, JSON, JSONL, Parquet, Excel, ACH,
and Fedwire files ((devel))

[Usage]
  sqly [OPTIONS] [FILE|DIRECTORY|URL ...]

  Each input is loaded into an in-memory SQLite database as a table named
  after the file. With --sql or --sql-file, sqly runs the query and exits;
  with neither, it opens an interactive shell, or runs SQL piped into it.
  sqly has no subcommands. Dot-commands are available inside the shell.

[Examples]
  - open the shell with a file loaded
    sqly sample.csv
  - run one query over a file, a directory, or a URL
    sqly --sql 'SELECT * FROM sample' sample.csv
  - join files of different formats
    sqly --sql 'SELECT * FROM a JOIN b USING (id)' a.csv b.parquet
  - write the result somewhere else, in another format
    sqly --output-format json --output out.json --sql 'SELECT 1' a.csv
  - read the data from a pipe and the query from a file
    cat data.csv | sqly --stdin-format csv --sql-file query.sql
  - look at a file you have never seen, as JSON
    sqly --inspect sample.csv

[Options]
  Input:
        --stdin-format FORMAT   read stdin as a dataset instead of as SQL; one
                                of: csv, tsv, ltsv, json, jsonl
        --stdin-table NAME      table name for the --stdin-format dataset
                                (default: stdin)
        --sheet NAME            import only this sheet from Excel (.xlsx) inputs
        --encoding ENCODING     decode CSV, TSV, LTSV, JSON, and JSONL inputs
                                that have no BOM as one of: utf-8, shift-jis,
                                euc-jp, iso-2022-jp, utf-16le, utf-16be
                                (default: utf-8)
        --row-mismatch POLICY   CSV/TSV only: handle a row whose field count
                                differs from the header, as one of: error (fail
                                the import), skip (drop the row), pad (pad a
                                short row, fail on a long one) (default: error)

  Query:
    -s, --sql SQL               run one SQL statement, then exit
    -f, --sql-file FILE         run every statement in this SQL file, printing
                                each result, then exit
        --dialect NAME          write the query in one of: sqlite, mysql,
                                postgresql, googlesql; sqly translates it to
                                SQLite (default: sqlite)

  Output:
    -o, --output FILE           write the query result to this file instead of
                                stdout; the run must produce exactly one result
        --output-format FORMAT  print the query result as one of: table,
                                vertical, csv, tsv, ltsv, json, jsonl, markdown,
                                excel, parquet; excel and parquet need --output
                                (default: table)

  Inspection:
        --inspect               print a JSON report of the imported tables
                                (schema, row counts, sample rows) and exit
        --inspect-sample N      sample rows per table in the --inspect report; 0
                                for schema only (default: 5)

  Write back:
        --save-tables DIR       after the run, write every table the session
                                changed into this directory, in its source
                                format; the sources are untouched
        --save-in-place         after the run, overwrite the source file of
                                every table the session changed (destructive)

  General:
    -h, --help                  print this help and exit
    -v, --version               print the sqly version and exit

Queries are SQLite by default; --dialect translates MySQL, PostgreSQL, or
GoogleSQL syntax into it.

Documentation:   https://nao1215.github.io/sqly/
Report an issue: https://github.com/nao1215/sqly/issues
